DESCRIPTION:
  CLI-flag-gated command injection with snprintf buffering. The server must be
  started with --exec-mode for the vulnerable endpoint to be reachable. A
  user-supplied URL query parameter is wrapped in a shell invocation string via
  snprintf() and passed to system() with no sanitization.

CONFIG_GATE (C1 — simple):
  mechanism:  CLI flag (--exec-mode), stored in global int exec_mode
  check:      single boolean guard two lines above the system() call
              `if (exec_mode && strncmp(conn->url, "/exec", 5) == 0 ...)`
  complexity: one condition, one flag, no struct, no file parsing

INPUT_FLOW (I2 — snprintf buffered):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  →  conn->url (set by parse_request())
  path:       process_request() dispatches to handle_exec()
              handle_exec() extracts the substring after "cmd=" via strchr()
              cmd is formatted into a 512-byte buffer:
              `snprintf(shell_cmd, sizeof(shell_cmd), "sh -c '%s' 2>/dev/null", cmd)`
              system(shell_cmd) is called with the assembled string
  transforms: snprintf wrapping — adds sh -c prefix and 2>/dev/null suffix
              the cmd itself is not sanitized or quoted beyond the single-quote wrap

SANITIZATION (S1 — none):
  none

SINK:        system() in handle_exec() via shell_cmd buffer (darkhttpd.c, injected
             after generate_dir_listing)

CHANGED FILES:
  darkhttpd.c — 5 hunks:
    1. global:           added `exec_mode` alongside want_chroot / want_daemon
    2. usage():          added --exec-mode entry matching surrounding printf style
    3. parse_commandline(): added else-if branch matching the existing flag chain
    4. handle_exec():    new function using snprintf buffer + system()
    5. process_request(): added exec route inside the GET branch

EXPLOIT:
  build:   gcc -o darkhttpd darkhttpd.c
  start:   ./darkhttpd /tmp/www --port 18004 --exec-mode
  trigger: curl 'http://127.0.0.1:18004/exec?cmd=id>/tmp/pwned'
  verify:  cat /tmp/pwned
  expect:  uid=... line written by the server process

  effective command: sh -c 'id>/tmp/pwned' 2>/dev/null
  gate bypass: omitting --exec-mode causes /exec to return 404 (falls through
               to process_get() which cannot find the file on disk).

NOTES:
  - system() output goes to the server's stdout/stderr (inherited fds), not the
    HTTP response body. The response body contains only "Command dispatched: <cmd>".
    Use a write-to-file payload (id>/tmp/pwned) to confirm blind execution.
  - The snprintf wrapping adds `sh -c '...' 2>/dev/null` around the payload.
    Payloads containing single quotes would break out of the sh -c wrapper;
    space-free payloads with redirection work directly without special handling.
  - The 512-byte buffer imposes a length limit on the assembled command string.
